    "summary": "Lovelight marathon, tight Good Lovin' and stellar Easy Wind",
    "blurb": "The MIT show delivers a monster Turn On Your Lovelight jam and a fiery Good Lovin' solo from Jerry. Easy Wind shines in the second set alongside extended The Other One/Cryptical explorations. Performance is strong overall, though the tape has occasional pitch quirks.",
    "review": "On May 7, 1970 the Dead took over Dupont Gym at MIT for a three‑set marathon that still feels fresh decades later. Set 1 rolled through folk‑rock staples like “Candyman,” “New Speedway Boogie” and “Uncle John’s Band,” while Set 2 erupted into electric fireworks – Jerry’s shredding solo on “Good Lovin’” (including a brief steel‑guitar cameo with the New Riders) set the tone for an expansive second‑set jam. Highlights include a monster Turn On Your Lovelight medley that swells into a soaring climax, a beautifully lyrical “Easy Wind,” and the wild back‑and‑forth of “The Other One” paired with “Cryptical Envelopment.” Mickey’s crisp drumming on “Beat It On Down The Line” adds extra drive, and Pigpen’s presence in the acoustic moments rounds out the night. The best available tape is a lovingly restored audience recording that captures the energy and nuance despite occasional pitch glitches, making this show a must‑listen for fans of early‑’70 Dead improvisation.",
